/// Default direction for a leg — twin of `getDefaultBuySellType:legType:trade:`
/// (`OKTArbStgyPOUtil.m:39`).
/// 
/// Native derives an intermediate side first (`legType == Right ? buy : sell`),
/// then maps it. In open-close mode the sell branch resolves to **`SellLong`
/// (close long)**, NOT `SellShort` — i.e. the left leg opens on "close long"
/// while the list's second entry is "open short". That is native's rule; it is
/// reproduced, not corrected.
pub fn arb_default_direction(inst_type: String, position_mode: String, leg: ArbLegType) -> ArbDirectionValue { todo!() }

/// Round-trips a `(posSide, side)` pair back to a direction identity — the twin
/// of `initWithPosSide:side:` (`OKTASDirectionListModel.m:26-48`).
/// 
/// `None` when `side` is neither buy nor sell. Native had no such guard: its
/// `else` branch treated any non-buy side as sell, so `("long", "")` produced
/// `SellLong`. Returning `None` instead keeps a missing side from
/// materialising as a real direction; every caller here already handles the
/// no-direction state, and inventing `SellLong` out of an empty side is the
/// kind of silent wrong-order defect this migration is meant to remove.
/// Recorded as a deliberate deviation.
